==English==

===Verb===
{{en-verb|*}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[double]] the quantity, amount or duration of something
#: {{ux|en|I'm going to '''double up''' my enlistment.}}
# {{lb|en|poker|by extension|intransitive}} To double one's amount of chips by winning an [[all-in]] pot.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|of a person}} To [[bend]], [[bend over]]; to [[fold]]; to [[stoop]].
#: {{nearsyn|en|double over}}
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Jekyll and Hyde|||"It is well, then that we should be frank," said the other. "We both think more than we have said; let us make a clean breast. This masked figure that you saw, did you recognise it?" / "Well, sir, it went so quick, and the creature was so '''doubled up''', that I could hardly swear to that," was the answer. "But if you mean, was it Mr. Hyde?--why, yes, I think it was!"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|title=Galloway|passage=If I could jump high enough out of the water to wedge my '''doubled-up''' fist in that crack I could hang by it. If I opened my hand I'd slide right back in the water, but if I could keep my fist closed {{...}}|year=1970|author=Louis L'Amour|page=43|isbn=0553127306|year_published=1974|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/galloway0000loui/page/43|publisher=Bantam Books|edition=new|url=https://archive.org/details/galloway0000loui}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause (someone) to bend over; to beat up (someone).
#: {{ux|en|I '''doubled''' him '''up''' with a swift punch to the stomach.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To have a secondary use.
#: {{syn|en|double}}
#: {{ux|en|This unfolding sofa '''doubles up''' as a bed.}}
#* {{RQ:Porter Pollyanna|chapter=7|passage=A little fearfully now, Pollyanna felt her way to these bags, selected a nice fat soft one (it contained Miss Polly's sealskin coat) for a bed; and a thinner one to be '''doubled up''' for a pillow, and still another (which was so thin it seemed almost empty) for a covering.}}
#* {{quote-journal
|en
|date=June 11, 2017
|author=Ben Fisher
|title=England seal Under-20 World Cup glory as Dominic Calvert-Lewin strikes
|work=the Guardian
|url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/jun/11/venezuela-england-under-20-world-cup-final-match-report
|page=
|passage={{w|Fikayo Tomori}}, the Chelsea defender, sang “championes, championes” with his winners’ medal swaying from side to side. For {{w|Joshua Onomah}} and {{w|Ainsley Maitland-Niles}}, England banners '''doubled up''' as celebratory bandanas.}}
# {{lb|en|baseball|transitive}} To get the second out in a [[double play]], typically referring to getting an out by beating a runner back to a base (often by throwing) after a fly ball has been caught
#: {{ux|en|Jones snared the [[liner]] and then stepped on the bag to '''double up''' the runner.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To employ double the usual resources for a particular purpose; to work in pairs.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2012|author=Ben Smith|title=Leeds United 2-1 Everton|url=http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/0/football/19632366|passage=Tactically smart, Leeds' work-rate was also admirable, their players often '''doubling up''' on Everton's main threats like Marouane Fellaini, while Victor Anichibe found he had unwelcome, unstinting company throughout.}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive|slang}} To marry or live with someone; to share a living space.

